+// MIGO: the Windows entry point for the callback table described at the
+// ValueSerializer delegate above. Placed last so every slot it mentions is
+// already declared.
+//
+// Registration is BY NAME rather than by a struct of ordered fields. An ordered
+// struct would put the same 24 entries in two independently maintained lists --
+// this file and the Rust side -- where getting the order wrong compiles cleanly
+// on both sides and only shows up as a call through the wrong pointer at run
+// time. Keying on the symbol name removes that failure mode entirely: a name
+// that does not match any slot is reported to the caller instead of silently
+// binding to the wrong one, and `v8__host_callbacks_ready` lets the host assert
+// every slot was filled before it initialises V8.
+//
+// The host must register all of these before initialising V8 and before any
+// ValueSerializer, ValueDeserializer, inspector or cppgc-managed Rust object is
+// used. Slots start null, so using one early faults rather than misbehaving.
+namespace {
+
+struct HostCallbackSlot {
+  const char* name;
+  // Address of the function-pointer variable. Held as void* and written with
+  // memcpy: assigning through a `void**` alias of a differently typed function
+  // pointer would be undefined behaviour even where it happens to work.
+  void* slot;
+};
+
